CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "carth-dido", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The Legend of Queen Elissa", era: "Traditional · 814 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "Carthage's founding story involves a murdered husband, a stolen fortune and a queen who outwits a king with a knife and a cow. None of it is history. All of it is worth knowing.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Flight from Tyre",
      text: ["The story goes like this. In the Phoenician city of **Tyre**, a king dies and leaves the throne to his son and daughter jointly. The son, Pygmalion, does not fancy sharing. He murders his sister's husband — who is also the high priest, and rich — and tries to keep the money quiet.",
        "The sister is **Elissa**, and she is not fooled. She loads the treasure onto ships, takes a group of nobles with her, and sails. On the way out she throws sacks overboard in full view of Pygmalion's men, announcing that she is committing her husband's gold to the sea. The sacks are full of sand. The gold is in the hold.",
        "She lands on the North African coast and asks the local ruler for land. He offers, with the air of a man being funny, as much ground as can be covered by a single oxhide. She has the hide cut into a thread so fine it encircles an entire hill — and that hill becomes the citadel of Carthage, which the Greeks called **Byrsa**, meaning oxhide.",
        "That last detail is almost certainly a joke that got fossilised. *Byrsa* is probably a Greek ear mishearing the Phoenician *bosra*, meaning citadel — and once you hear \"oxhide\", you need a story about an oxhide. Ancient etymology worked backwards from the word to the tale far more often than anyone admitted."],
      key: "Tradition says Elissa (Dido) fled Tyre after her brother murdered her husband, and won Carthage's citadel hill by a trick with an oxhide." },
    { title: "The bit Rome added later",
      text: ["Now the part everybody actually knows: Dido and Aeneas. She falls in love with a Trojan refugee, he abandons her because the gods have plans for him in Italy, and she builds a pyre, curses his descendants and kills herself.",
        "Here is the thing. That is not a Carthaginian story. It is not even an old story. **Virgil** wrote it into the *Aeneid* for Augustus, more than a hundred years after Roman soldiers had levelled the city and salted the argument for good.",
        "And look at what it does. It makes the founder of Carthage a woman destroyed by love for a Roman. It makes her dying curse the reason for the Punic Wars — so three enormous conflicts stop being about Sicily, silver and grain and become destiny, personal, and inevitable. Rome did not defeat Carthage in that version. Rome fulfilled a prophecy.",
        "It is a superb piece of writing and one of the most effective retrospective justifications any empire has produced. Just do not mistake it for Carthage's opinion of itself."],
      key: "The Dido and Aeneas romance is a Roman literary invention by Virgil, written centuries after Carthage fell." },
    { title: "What the ground says",
      text: ["So: legend, myth, propaganda. Is any of it true?",
        "Some of it, and here is where founding stories usually fall apart — this one does not. Excavation under the city puts the earliest Phoenician material at roughly **800 BC**, which is close enough to the traditional 814 to be genuinely surprising. Most legendary founding dates are centuries adrift. Rome's is.",
        "The wider shape holds too. Carthage really was a Tyrian colony, planted on a coast the Phoenicians were already working, at a moment when Tyre was under pressure from Assyria and had good reason to be establishing daughter cities somewhere safer. A colony founded by an exiled royal faction fleeing a succession fight is not remotely implausible. It is what that world did.",
        "What we cannot check is Elissa herself. No inscription names her. No Carthaginian text about her survives, because almost no Carthaginian text survives at all.",
        "And the daughter outlived the mother. Tyre was taken by Alexander in 332 BC. By then Carthage had inherited the entire western Phoenician trade network and had been the great power of the western Mediterranean for two hundred years."],
      key: "Archaeology dates Carthage's founding to c. 800 BC; it outlived Tyre and inherited the Phoenician trade network." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Where does the story of Dido and Aeneas come from?", options: ["Contemporary Phoenician records", "Virgil's Aeneid, centuries later", "Carthaginian temple inscriptions", "Polybius"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is Roman literature written after Carthage's destruction, and it turns three wars over trade into personal destiny." },
    { q: "What does archaeology say about the traditional founding date of 814 BC?", options: ["It is centuries too early", "It is broadly consistent with the evidence", "The site was uninhabited until 300 BC", "The site was Greek before it was Phoenician"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Unusually for a founding legend, the traditional date sits close to what the ground supports. Rome's does not." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-place", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The City and Its Neighbours", era: "c. 800 – 500 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Put your finger on a map where Carthage sat and you have most of its foreign policy already. Almost everything the city did for six hundred years follows from that one piece of coastline.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The best address in the Mediterranean",
      text: ["The Mediterranean has a waist. Between Cape Bon in Tunisia and the western tip of Sicily the sea narrows to about ninety miles, and there is a shallow bank running most of the way across it. Everything sailing between the eastern and western halves of that sea has to pass through the gap.",
        "Carthage sat on the southern side of it, on a promontory with sheltered water on two sides. That is the whole strategic story in one sentence: whoever holds this can see, tax, escort or stop the traffic of the entire Mediterranean.",
        "And they built the harbour to match. The **cothon** was two artificial basins, dug out rather than found. The outer one was rectangular and commercial and busy and visible. Behind it, connected by a channel you could close, was a circular naval harbour with an island in the middle, ringed by covered ship-sheds — somewhere in the region of two hundred and twenty berths, with the admiral's building on the island.",
        "The clever bit is what you could not see. From the merchant harbour, or from the sea, you had no idea how many warships were in there or how ready they were. Appian says a screen wall meant traders could not look in. Carthage could be arming for a war or empty, and you would find out when the ships came out.",
        "Both basins are still traceable from the air today, which is a fairly unusual thing to be able to say about an ancient naval installation."],
      key: "Carthage controlled the Mediterranean's narrowest channel and built the cothon, a double harbour with a hidden naval basin." },
    { title: "Who else was in the neighbourhood",
      text: ["Carthage was never alone, and its neighbours explain most of its decisions.",
        "Immediately inland were the **Libyans**, farming the best land in North Africa — and taxed savagely for the privilege, up to half the harvest in wartime. Remember that figure. It is going to matter enormously later.",
        "West and south were the **Numidian** kingdoms, who produced the finest light cavalry in the western world. Carthage hired them for centuries. Rome eventually hired them instead, and that switch decides a war.",
        "North across the channel was **Sicily**, half Greek and permanently contested. East along the coast were the other Phoenician cities. In Italy the **Etruscans** were allies against Greek expansion — and behind the Etruscans, unremarked for a long time, was a small inland town called Rome.",
        "And then west, out past the Pillars of Hercules, was the thing nobody else had: the Atlantic. Tin from Brittany and Cornwall, gold from West Africa, salted fish from the Atlantic coast. Carthage guarded that route the way a company guards a patent, and Strabo reports a Carthaginian captain who deliberately wrecked his own ship on a shoal rather than let a Roman vessel follow him to the tin islands. The state compensated him for the loss.",
        "That is not a colourful anecdote. That is a trade policy."],
      key: "Carthage was ringed by Libyans, Numidian cavalry kingdoms, Greek Sicily, Etruscans and later Rome — and controlled the Atlantic trade route." },
    { title: "How it was actually run",
      text: ["Carthage was a republic, and a good one. **Aristotle** puts it in his *Politics* alongside Sparta and Crete as a well-governed state — which is a remarkable thing for a Greek to say about a non-Greek city, and he says it while Carthage and the Greeks are actively fighting over Sicily.",
        "Two chief magistrates called **suffetes** were elected annually. There was a senate of the wealthy, a council of 104 that audited generals, and popular assemblies with real powers when the senate was split. Monarchy had been abandoned early. Power sat with people who owned ships.",
        "Now the flaw, and it is the one that eventually kills the city. Carthaginian citizens did not, as a rule, fight. The army was **hired**: Libyan levies, Numidian horse, Iberian and Gallic infantry, Balearic slingers, Greek officers.",
        "In fairness, this is a sensible arrangement for a trading state. Your citizens are more valuable making money than dying in Sicily, and mercenaries are only expensive while you are using them. It also produced armies with genuinely excellent specialists, and Carthage won a great deal with them.",
        "But it has two failure modes, and Carthage found both. A hired army cannot absorb a catastrophe — lose it and you have to buy another one, and the market may not have one in stock. And an unpaid hired army does not go home quietly.",
        "Rome's system was the mirror image. Roman armies were citizens with farms, which made them slower, less specialised and vastly harder to destroy permanently. After Cannae, Rome raised more legions. Carthage, after a comparable defeat, had to negotiate."],
      key: "Carthage was a republic of elected suffetes and a merchant senate that fought with mercenaries rather than citizens." },
    { title: "The Tophet, and a warning about your sources",
      text: ["There is a walled precinct at Carthage containing thousands of small urns. Each holds the cremated remains of an infant or a young animal, and each is marked with a stone. It is called the **Tophet**, and it is the single most argued-about site in Punic archaeology.",
        "The Greek and Roman sources are lurid and unanimous: Carthaginians burned their own children alive to the god Baal Hammon, in numbers, especially when frightened. Diodorus describes a bronze statue with sloping arms that rolled the child into a fire pit.",
        "So — did they? Genuinely **Contested**, and the argument is live. One side reads the urns as exactly what the sources describe. The other reads them as a cemetery for infants who died naturally, which in the ancient world would be a great many, buried in a sacred precinct with an offering. Both camps point at the same bones. The tooth and skeletal evidence has been used to argue both ways.",
        "But hold on to the deeper problem here, because it applies to everything else in this Set. Punic literature is essentially gone. Carthage had libraries; after 146 BC the Romans handed them to their Numidian allies and they vanished. What survives about Carthage was written by Greeks and Romans who fought it, and Rome had spent a century arguing that these people were a species of monster who could not be lived alongside.",
        "That is not an occasional bias you can spot and correct for. It shapes the entire surviving picture. Every claim in this Set carries a discount, and the ones that most flatter Rome carry the largest."],
      key: "Child sacrifice at the Tophet is genuinely contested; almost all surviving evidence about Carthage was written by its enemies." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was the cothon?", options: ["A Carthaginian law code", "An artificial double harbour with a hidden naval basin", "The council of elders", "A type of warship"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The circular naval basin behind the merchant harbour meant nobody could see how many warships Carthage had ready." },
    { q: "What was the structural weakness of Carthage's military system?", options: ["It had no navy", "It relied on hired troops rather than citizens", "Its generals were elected annually", "It banned cavalry"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A hired army cannot absorb a catastrophe, and an unpaid one does not go home quietly. Carthage found both out." },
    { q: "Why must claims about Carthage carry a discount?", options: ["The sources are undated", "Punic literature is largely lost, so the record comes from Carthage's enemies", "Carthaginians did not write", "All sources are medieval"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is a systematic bias, not an occasional one — and the claims that most flatter Rome carry the largest discount." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-sicily", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "The Sicilian Wars", era: "580 – 265 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Three hundred years of on-and-off war over one island. Carthage never won it, never lost it, and never let it go — and almost nobody remembers it happened.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Why anyone cared about Sicily",
      text: ["Look at Sicily on a map and it is doing two things at once. It is the biggest island in the Mediterranean and it grows grain in serious quantity, which in the ancient world is the same as saying it prints money. And it sits directly across the sea lane Carthage lived on. Hold all of it and you can close the channel.",
        "The trouble was that it had been settled from both ends, like two families moving into opposite ends of the same house. Greek colonists took the east and south from the eighth century BC — Syracuse, Acragas, Gela, Himera. Phoenicians held the west from **Motya**, **Panormus** and **Lilybaeum**, ports that looked south toward Africa and were a day's sail from it. In the middle were the Sicels and Elymians, who get almost no lines in this story because both of their neighbours wrote more than they did.",
        "Here is the deadlock, and it is worth getting straight because everything else follows from it. Neither side could accept the other holding the whole island. Neither side could actually take it. Carthage could always land an army; it could never keep one supplied indefinitely in hostile country. Syracuse could beat a Carthaginian army in the field; it could never chase it across the water and finish the job in Africa.",
        "So the argument ran for three centuries — longer than the United States has existed — in bursts of appalling violence separated by treaties that drew a line across the island and then quietly stopped working. It is the longest-running war in the ancient Mediterranean. It is also almost forgotten, for the simple reason that both of the states fighting it were later destroyed by a third one that wrote the books."],
      key: "Sicily's grain and position made it a permanent, unresolvable contest between Carthage and the Greek cities." },
    { title: "Himera, and a coincidence that did a lot of work",
      text: ["The first big Carthaginian disaster came at **Himera** in 480 BC, against Syracuse and Acragas together. Herodotus tells us the Carthaginian commander **Hamilcar** spent the entire battle at an altar sacrificing, and when he looked up and saw his army coming apart, threw himself into the fire.",
        "Now, Greek tradition put Himera on the very same day as **Salamis**. Think about how useful that is if you are Greek. One afternoon; two ends of the world; Greeks in the east hold off Persia, Greeks in the west hold off Carthage, and civilisation survives by providence. It is a wonderful story and it is almost certainly a later construction. This app files it as **Interpretation**. It tells you an enormous amount about how Greeks wished to see themselves and precisely nothing about what Carthage thought it was doing — which, on the evidence, was protecting a trading position at the request of an allied Sicilian tyrant. Rather less cosmic.",
        "The defeat kept Carthage out of Sicily for seventy years, and there is a good argument that the pause redirected the whole state. Blocked to the north, it turned inland and outward instead: taxing the Libyan farmland properly, and pushing fleets out into the Atlantic.",
        "When the war restarted in 409 BC it ran on and off for another century and a half. Against **Dionysius I of Syracuse**, who besieged Motya and cracked it open with catapults — a brand new weapon — and then lost most of what he had won. Against **Timoleon**. And then against **Agathocles**, who in 310 BC was shut up in Syracuse under blockade and did the thing nobody saw coming.",
        "He left. Took his army out by sea, landed in **Africa**, and burned his own ships on the beach so that every man with him understood there was no way back. He tore up Carthaginian territory for three years and came genuinely close to taking the city.",
        "He failed in the end, and abandoned his own army to save himself, which is not a great look. But he had proved something important: hit Carthage at home and it panics. A Roman would read that later and take notes."],
      key: "Defeat at Himera (480 BC) checked Carthage in Sicily for seventy years; Agathocles later took the war to Africa in 310 BC." },
    { title: "What the fighting was actually about",
      text: ["Underneath three centuries of siege and slaughter sat something much less dramatic and much more important. Carthage was not trying to build a Sicilian empire. It was protecting a monopoly.",
        "The treaties with Rome are the giveaway, and we have them because Polybius saw the bronze originals and copied them out. The earliest one specifies where Roman ships may and may not sail, requires that any trading they do in Carthaginian territory happen in front of a herald or notary, and flatly forbids them from sailing past a fixed cape at all. A later one bars Romans from trading in Sardinia and Libya entirely and from founding anything in Sicily.",
        "Read those as diplomacy between equals and they make no sense. Read them as what they are — a schedule of permissions issued by the party that controls the water — and they make perfect sense.",
        "What those permissions were protecting: West African gold, Iberian silver, Sardinian grain, purple dye worth more by weight than silver, and above all the carrying trade. Being the ship that everybody else's goods travel in is quieter than conquest and considerably more profitable.",
        "Which means Carthage never needed to rule Sicily. It needed nobody else to rule Sicily. That is a much lower bar, and for three hundred years it cleared it.",
        "The bar became unclearable when a new kind of state showed up. When **Pyrrhus of Epirus** campaigned through Italy and Sicily in the 270s BC and briefly looked like unifying the island, Carthage and Rome found themselves fighting on the same side — the last time that ever happened. Pyrrhus, sailing away, is supposed to have looked back at Sicily and remarked what a fine wrestling ground they were leaving behind for Rome and Carthage. He was right within a decade."],
      key: "Carthage fought to keep the western Mediterranean closed to competitors; its treaties with Rome were schedules of permission, not agreements between equals." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why could neither Carthage nor the Greeks settle the Sicilian question?", options: ["Neither could conquer the whole island, and neither could accept the other holding it", "A treaty forbade fighting", "Rome intervened each time", "The island was too poor to fight over"], correct: 0,
      explain: "Structural deadlock, not lack of effort — which is exactly why the wars keep recurring across three centuries." },
    { q: "How does the app classify the tradition that Himera was fought on the same day as Salamis?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "Interpretation — it reflects Greek self-image", "Unknown"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The framing serves a Greek story about civilisation being defended at both ends of the world. It is a reading, not a verified synchronism." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-hanno", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "Hanno the Navigator", era: "c. 500 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "A Carthaginian fleet sails off the edge of the Greek map — and files a report we can still read today. It is very nearly the only Carthaginian voice we have.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Turn left at the Pillars",
      text: ["Around 500 BC a suffete named **Hanno** took a fleet out through the Pillars of Hercules — Gibraltar — and instead of turning right for the known world, turned left and went down the Atlantic coast of Africa.",
        "The stated purpose was colonisation. The account claims sixty ships and thirty thousand settlers, which is certainly inflated, though it does tell you the scale they wanted people to imagine. It names the towns founded along what is now Morocco, and then it keeps going south.",
        "And here is the genuinely remarkable part: we can read his report. The ***Periplus of Hanno*** survives, as a Greek translation of a Punic text that was apparently put up on a tablet in the temple of Baal at Carthage. It is a handful of pages in flat first-person plural — we sailed, we saw, we founded — and it is close to the only thing we have that a Carthaginian actually wrote about Carthaginian business.",
        "What it describes gets steadily stranger. Coastal lagoons with elephants in them. Crocodiles and hippos in a river. People in animal skins throwing stones at the ships. Then, one night, a whole coastline on fire, with drums and pipes and shouting coming off it, which unnerved the crews enough that they kept sailing. Then a mountain pouring flame into the sea, which they named the **Chariot of the Gods**.",
        "And finally an island in a gulf full of hairy people whom their Libyan interpreters called ***gorillai***. The women could not be caught. Three were taken, fought so hard they had to be killed, and their skins were carried back to Carthage. In 1847 an American missionary went looking for a name for a newly described ape and borrowed that word straight out of the *Periplus*. Which is why the animal walking around your local zoo is wearing a Punic name written down by a Carthaginian admiral twenty-five centuries ago."],
      key: "Hanno the Navigator sailed the Atlantic coast of Africa c. 500 BC; his Periplus survives as a Greek translation of a Punic text." },
    { title: "How far did he actually get?",
      text: ["**Contested**, and the honest range of expert opinion is embarrassingly wide: southern Morocco if you are cautious, Sierra Leone or even Gabon if you are not.",
        "The whole argument turns on matching things he describes — a volcano, a gulf, a river with crocodiles — to real coastline. And the further south you push, the more the case depends on which volcano you are willing to accept. Mount Cameroon is the ambitious candidate. It is also two and a half thousand miles from home in a galley.",
        "Some scholars go further and doubt the voyage as written altogether. On that reading the text is part genuine log, part advertisement: a state showing off the reach of its navy, or possibly making the route sound so unpleasant that nobody else tries it. The hairy islanders might be chimpanzees, or baboons, or a real people described by frightened sailors through two layers of translation.",
        "What is not in dispute is the pattern the voyage belongs to. Carthage also sent **Himilco** the other way — north up the Atlantic face of Europe, toward Brittany and probably Britain, chasing tin. No tin, no bronze; it was worth an ocean voyage. Fragments of that account survive too, and they describe a sea choked with weed, no wind at all, and monsters. Which is either a genuine observation of Sargasso-type water, or exactly the sort of thing you would write if you wanted competitors to stay home. Given what we know about Carthaginian trade policy, I would not rule out the second.",
        "Either way: this is a state systematically exploring and settling ocean coastline, with public funding and written records, at a time when Greek geography was still mostly hugging the Mediterranean shore. It is the clearest single measure of what was lost when Carthage's libraries were dispersed."],
      key: "How far Hanno sailed is contested, but Carthage clearly ran systematic state-funded Atlantic exploration, including Himilco's northern voyage." },
    { title: "An empire of harbours",
      text: ["This is the thing most easily missed about Carthage, because it is the exact opposite of how Rome worked.",
        "Carthage did not expand by conquest. What it planted was a chain of coastal footholds: a sheltered anchorage, a warehouse, a temple, a market, and a working arrangement with whoever ran the interior. It wanted the harbour. It did not particularly want the hinterland.",
        "That approach is cheap, it scales fast, it needs very few of your own citizens, and — crucially for us — it leaves almost nothing behind. No road network. No colonial cities laid out on a grid. No monumental inscriptions announcing the conquest of anybody. Which is a large part of why Carthage is archaeologically so much thinner than its importance deserves, and why the popular picture of the ancient western Mediterranean is so lopsidedly Roman. We are partly just seeing whoever built in stone and wrote on it.",
        "By the mid-third century BC the network ran from the Atlantic coast of Morocco through southern Iberia, the Balearics, Sardinia and western Sicily. On paper Carthage was the dominant power of the western Mediterranean, richer than any Greek state, with a navy nobody could match.",
        "Its weakness was its strength turned inside out. An empire of harbours has no depth. There is no interior to fall back into. It can be unpicked one port at a time by anyone patient enough to spend the years, and it depends absolutely on controlling the water in between the links.",
        "In 264 BC Rome got involved in a squabble at Messana about a garrison, and began — without having the faintest intention of doing so — doing precisely that."],
      key: "Carthage expanded through chains of coastal trading footholds rather than territorial conquest, which left it rich but without depth." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What is the Periplus of Hanno?", options: ["A Roman forgery of the 1st century AD", "A Greek translation of a Punic account of an Atlantic voyage", "A Carthaginian law code", "A Greek epic poem"], correct: 1,
      explain: "One of very few Punic texts to survive in any form — and only because it passed through Greek on the way." },
    { q: "What characterised the Punic colonial model?", options: ["Mass settlement of farmers inland", "Chains of coastal trading footholds with local arrangements", "Direct military occupation", "Religious conversion of neighbours"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Harbours and warehouses rather than provinces. Cheap and flexible — and archaeologically almost invisible." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-after-first", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "What the First War Cost", era: "264 – 237 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The war itself lives in the Wars tab. This chapter is about the bill that arrived afterwards — because the bill is what causes the next war.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Twenty-three years, in one paragraph",
      text: ["In 264 BC Rome put a garrison into **Messana** in Sicily. A local squabble between mercenaries turned into a war that ran for twenty-three years: the longest continuous war in ancient history, and by some measures the biggest naval war fought anywhere before the modern era.",
        "Carthage started it holding total command of the sea, with three centuries of naval tradition behind it, against an opponent that essentially had no ships. It finished it with no fleet, no Sicily and a debt. That is quite a reversal.",
        "How it happened — the copied warship, the corvus, whole fleets lost in storms, the last battle at the Aegates Islands — is in the **First Punic War** entry under Wars, which opens once you have studied both societies up to 264 BC. I am deliberately not telling it twice, and telling it from one side only would be worse than not telling it at all.",
        "What matters here is the invoice."],
      key: "The First Punic War (264–241 BC) cost Carthage Sicily, its naval dominance and a heavy indemnity." },
    { title: "The bill",
      text: ["Three thousand two hundred talents over ten years, on top of the direct cost of building and losing fleet after fleet. And Sicily went — its western half, held for something like four hundred years, became Rome's first overseas province, and was promptly taxed to help pay for the next war. Read that sentence again; it is not a small detail.",
        "Now think about what that does to a state built the way Carthage was. Its entire military rested on hiring soldiers. Hiring soldiers rests on revenue. Carthage had just lost a large chunk of the revenue and taken on the debt in the same document, and now had to service an indemnity, pay the army it already had, and rebuild a navy — with the Sicilian ports gone and the sea no longer reliably its own.",
        "But the political damage was worse than the money, and this is the part that decides everything afterwards. **Hamilcar Barca** had held the mountains of western Sicily for years. He had never been beaten in the field. He was still holding when the government negotiated a peace over his head and instructed him to arrange the evacuation. He obeyed. He also resigned rather than hand over the weapons himself.",
        "So the war produced a state that had been defeated and a general who had not. Those two things do not live comfortably in the same country. Everything in the next fifty years of Carthaginian history grows out of the gap between them."],
      key: "Carthage lost Sicily and took on an indemnity while its undefeated field commander came home to a peace he rejected." },
    { title: "And then Sardinia",
      text: ["The worst of it arrived three years later, right in the middle of the mercenary revolt that is the subject of the next chapter.",
        "With Carthage fighting for its life inside its own territory, the Carthaginian garrison on **Sardinia** mutinied in sympathy and appealed to Rome. Rome had turned down almost exactly that appeal once before, correctly, as a breach of the treaty. This time it said yes. And when Carthage started preparing to go and get the island back, Rome announced that these preparations were themselves an act of war against Rome, and offered peace on terms: hand over Sardinia and Corsica, and pay another 1,200 talents.",
        "Carthage — with an enemy army in sight of its own walls and no capacity whatsoever to fight a second one — agreed.",
        "Now, you might expect that to be a Carthaginian complaint we hear at second hand and should discount. It is not. **Polybius** is our witness, and Polybius is Greek, wrote inside the household of the Roman general who destroyed Carthage, and is nowhere in his history remotely sentimental about Carthaginians. On this he says flatly that the seizure was contrary to all justice, that Rome had no reasonable ground for it whatever, and that it was one of the principal causes of the war that followed.",
        "When a source that partial concedes his own side was in the wrong, that concession is worth far more than any hostile witness's accusation. Hold on to that as a habit — it is one of the most useful tools you have for reading ancient history.",
        "The Carthaginians did not forget Sardinia. One family in particular did not forget it."],
      key: "Rome seized Sardinia and Corsica during Carthage's mercenary crisis; even Polybius calls it unjust and names it a cause of the next war." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why does the app put the battles of the First Punic War in a separate Wars entry?", options: ["They are unimportant", "So each side's own history stays about that society, not the fighting", "The sources are missing", "To reduce loading time"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Wars are shared events between two societies, so the app gives them their own subject rather than duplicating them in both syllabi." },
    { q: "What does Polybius say about Rome's seizure of Sardinia?", options: ["That it was lawful under treaty", "That it was contrary to justice and a cause of the next war", "That it never happened", "That Carthage requested it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A pro-Roman source conceding Roman fault is unusually strong evidence — which is exactly why it carries weight here." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-truceless", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Truceless War", era: "241 – 237 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The most savage war Carthage ever fought was not against Rome. It was against its own army, and it very nearly finished the city off.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Unpaid",
      text: ["The army came home from Sicily in 241 BC expecting years of back pay. Carthage was broke. So it made a decision that looks perfectly reasonable on paper and turned out to be nearly fatal: instead of paying, it moved the troops out of the city to a holding camp, kept them together in one place, and opened negotiations to settle the debt at a discount.",
        "There were around twenty thousand of them, and the only thing they all had in common was that Carthage owed them money. Otherwise they were Libyans, Iberians, Gauls, Ligurians, Greeks and Balearic islanders with no shared language at all. Polybius notes that officers could not address the whole force in any one tongue, so every message had to go through interpreters — who could be suspected of shading it, and were.",
        "Picture that for a moment. Twenty thousand armed, unpaid, bored professional soldiers, camped together, being haggled with in a language most of them cannot follow, by people they already suspect of cheating them. They mutinied under **Spendius**, an escaped Campanian slave who understood that any negotiated settlement put him back on a cross, and **Mathos**, a Libyan who knew the same thing was coming for his own people.",
        "And then it stopped being a pay dispute. The revolt spread out into the **Libyan** countryside, where Carthage had been taking half the harvest to fund the war, and where the resentment was three generations deep. The Libyans supplied men, food and money — Polybius says the women handed over their jewellery — and Carthage suddenly found a veteran army of perhaps seventy thousand in its own back garden, with almost no citizen soldiers of its own, having spent four centuries carefully arranging never to need any."],
      key: "In 241 BC Carthage's unpaid mercenary army mutinied and drew in the Libyan countryside, threatening the city itself." },
    { title: "Why 'truceless'",
      text: ["What makes this war different from every other war in the Set is that both sides deliberately destroyed the possibility of ending it.",
        "The rebel leadership had a specific tactical problem. Their men could always be bought back, and Carthage was rich. Spendius solved it by arranging the murder of the hostages — the general **Gisco** and around seven hundred Carthaginians, whose hands were cut off and legs broken before they were thrown into a pit alive — and then announcing that any future envoy would get the same treatment.",
        "That was not rage. That was policy, and it is chillingly effective policy. After that, no man in the rebel army could open negotiations, because there was nothing left for Carthage to forgive. Spendius had welded his own army to himself with an atrocity.",
        "Carthage replied in kind: prisoners trampled under elephants, and no captives taken thereafter.",
        "**Polybius** knew a very great deal about ancient warfare, and he calls this the most savage and unprincipled war of which he had any knowledge. He also gives it the name it still carries — the war *without a herald*. A herald was the mechanism by which ancient armies talked to each other. Both sides had killed theirs.",
        "It is also the sharpest possible demonstration of what Carthage's whole system actually cost. Rome went through the same postwar bankruptcy at the same moment and faced nothing remotely like this, for one reason: Roman soldiers were citizens with farms, who could be sent home and made to wait for their money."],
      key: "Both sides made quarter impossible; Polybius called it the most savage war he knew and named it the Truceless War." },
    { title: "Hamilcar's rescue, and the conclusion he drew",
      text: ["Eventually Carthage turned back to the man it had sidelined. **Hamilcar Barca** was given a command — alongside a rival general, because the Council still did not trust him — and the two of them disagreed so completely that the army was invited to choose between them. That is not remotely how the Council of 104 liked to run things, and it tells you how desperate matters had become.",
        "He won the war by 237 BC, and he won it by manoeuvre rather than by force. Which is impressive, because he was fighting men who had served under him in Sicily, knew exactly how he operated, and knew the ground as well as he did. He beat them anyway, by repeatedly getting rebel armies into country they could not get out of — most notoriously at the place Polybius calls the Saw, a defile where a trapped force starved, ate its own prisoners and then its slaves, and was destroyed when it finally came out. Spendius was crucified within sight of the city. Mathos was flogged through the streets of Carthage by the relatives of the men he had killed.",
        "Then Hamilcar sat down and worked out what had gone wrong, and his conclusions were structural rather than personal. Carthage's government had lost a war it could have won. It had signed a peace over its own general's head. It had then failed to pay the army that peace released, and had nearly been destroyed by the consequences. Any army belonging to that government would be underpaid at the worst imaginable moment, forever.",
        "So the next army would not belong to it. It would be raised abroad, paid out of resources he controlled personally, commanded by his own family, and stationed far enough away that the Council of 104 would be shown results rather than asked about plans.",
        "He looked west at **Iberia**: silver, manpower, and two thousand kilometres of distance between himself and the Carthaginian senate. He took his nine-year-old son with him."],
      key: "Hamilcar ended the revolt in 237 BC and concluded that any future army must be funded and commanded independently of Carthage." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What caused the Truceless War?", options: ["A religious dispute", "Carthage delaying payment to its returning mercenary army", "A Roman invasion", "A Numidian succession crisis"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The mercenary system's basic failure mode — an army with no citizenship stake, and a state that could not pay it." },
    { q: "What lesson did Hamilcar take from the revolt?", options: ["That mercenaries should be replaced with citizens", "That any future army must be funded and commanded independently of Carthage's government", "That Carthage should disarm", "That Rome could be trusted"], correct: 1,
      explain: "This is the direct origin of the semi-private Barcid empire in Spain." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-spain", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Conquest of Spain", era: "237 – 219 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "A beaten state goes out and builds a better empire than the one it lost — and then hands it to a family instead of a government. You can see the next war coming from here.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Iberia",
      text: ["Spring, 237 BC. **Hamilcar Barca** is sacrificing before leaving Carthage for **Iberia**, and his nine-year-old son is begging to come along.",
        "Polybius has this scene from Hannibal himself, told decades later to King Antiochus of Syria by way of explaining why he could never be trusted to make a lasting peace with Rome. His father led him to the altar, laid his hand on the victim, and made him swear he would never be a friend to the Roman people. Then he took him.",
        "Hamilcar had spent the Truceless War hunting down mercenaries he had once commanded, and he crossed to Spain with an army the state had not paid for and could not easily call back. He worked upward from **Gades**, the old Phoenician colony out on the Atlantic, and over nine years took the south and east by a mixture of siege, pitched battle, marriage and hiring. Iberian nobles came into his service with their warbands; their communities came into tribute. Where he could not conquer, he recruited. It is a very Carthaginian way to build an empire.",
        "And underneath all of it: **silver**. The mines of the Sierra Morena and the hills behind modern Cartagena were among the richest deposits anywhere in the ancient world. Polybius went and looked at them himself, and describes forty thousand men working the shafts near New Carthage and a daily yield to the treasury of twenty-five thousand drachmas. Slaves cut the galleries, water-screws drained them, and the metal went out through the harbour in ingots. This is a money-printing operation with an army attached.",
        "Hamilcar died in 228 BC, drowned in a river. Diodorus says it happened while he was drawing an enemy force away from his sons; Livy just says in battle. The accounts agree on nothing except that he did not come back.",
        "Look at the ledger, though. In nine years a defeated, bankrupt state had rebuilt its finances, its army and its fleet. And it had done all of it through a project that belonged, in every practical sense, to one family."],
      key: "Hamilcar conquered southern Iberia from 237 BC; its silver rebuilt Carthaginian finances and funded a standing army." },
    { title: "New Carthage",
      text: ["Command went to **Hasdrubal the Fair**, Hamilcar's son-in-law and a far better diplomat than he was a soldier. Where Hamilcar had taken Iberia by campaigning, Hasdrubal took most of the rest by treaty and marriage — he married an Iberian princess himself, and according to Diodorus the Iberians acclaimed him as their supreme general. Which is a title no Carthaginian magistrate had any business holding, and everyone involved knew it.",
        "In 227 BC he founded **Qart Hadasht** — New City, the exact same name Carthage itself carried, which tells you how modest his ambitions were. Romans called it **Carthago Nova**; today it is Cartagena. The site is one of the best in the western Mediterranean: a deep bay screened by an island, a peninsula joined to the mainland by a bar that only shows at low water, and hills that will take a wall on every side. Into it Hasdrubal put a palace, a mint, arsenals, dockyards and the entire administration of the silver.",
        "So: a capital. Four hundred miles by sea from the council that was theoretically running it.",
        "The coins make the same point more quietly, and I love this detail. Barcid silver struck in Spain carries a male head dressed as the god Melqart, club over the shoulder — and the face changes as the commanders change. Carthage had never in its history put a living man on its money.",
        "Hasdrubal was murdered in 221 BC inside his own palace, stabbed by a Celt whose master he had executed. Livy says the man was taken and tortured and kept the same expression throughout, as though he had already got what he came for. The army in Spain did not write home for instructions. It acclaimed **Hannibal**, aged about twenty-five, and Carthage then ratified what the army had already decided.",
        "By this point the Barcids held territory, revenue, a capital, a fleet and an army recruited locally and sworn personally to them. What they did not hold was a mandate. The council back in Carthage could approve or disapprove. It could not recall."],
      key: "Hasdrubal founded Carthago Nova in 227 BC as a Barcid capital; Hannibal took command by acclamation in 221 BC." },
    { title: "A line on a map, and the town on the wrong side of it",
      text: ["Rome watched all this with alarm and no free hands. The Gauls of the Po valley were massing for the invasion that ended at **Telamon** in 225 BC, and Illyrian piracy had already cost two wars. What Rome could afford right then was a line on a map. Around 226 BC its envoys and Hasdrubal agreed the **Ebro treaty**, conventionally read as fixing the river Ebro as the northern limit of Carthaginian expansion.",
        "**Saguntum** is where it all comes apart. The town sat well south of the Ebro — deep inside the zone Rome had just conceded — and it held an alliance with Rome. Was that alliance made before the treaty or after it? Disputed in antiquity, unresolvable now, and it happens to be the precise point on which the whole question of who broke faith turns. Convenient for everybody, in that it lets both sides be certain they were in the right.",
        "Hannibal besieged Saguntum in 219 BC. It held for eight months, which was long enough for embassies to travel and accomplish nothing at all. He was badly wounded at the wall by a javelin, and when the town finally fell Livy says some of its people burned their possessions, and themselves, rather than be taken.",
        "Rome demanded Hannibal be handed over. Livy stages the reply as pure theatre: the envoy Quintus Fabius gathered up a fold of his toga and told the Carthaginian council he was carrying peace and war in it, and would let out whichever they preferred. They told him to let out whichever he liked. He shook out war. They said they accepted it. Whether or not one word of that was ever spoken, the war started in 218 BC.",
        "Who was responsible is **Contested**, and it was contested by the ancient writers themselves. Polybius names three causes: Hamilcar's unextinguished anger, the Barcid success in Spain that made a second war thinkable at all, and Rome's seizure of Sardinia during the Truceless War — which, remember, he himself calls contrary to all justice. He blames Barcid ambition and concedes the Carthaginian grievance in the same breath.",
        "Both can be true at once. That is usually the answer with war-guilt questions, and the treaty had been drafted loosely enough for each side to be genuinely convinced it was inside the lines."],
      key: "Hannibal's siege of Saguntum in 219 BC triggered the Second Punic War; responsibility for the treaty breach is genuinely contested." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What funded the Barcid recovery in Spain?", options: ["Roman subsidies", "Iberian silver mines", "Egyptian grain", "Sicilian tribute"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Silver paid the indemnity, the army and the fleet — a state-scale revenue stream controlled by a single family." },
    { q: "Why was the status of Saguntum so contentious?", options: ["It was north of the Ebro", "It was Greek", "It lay inside the Carthaginian zone but held a Roman alliance", "It had no walls"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The timing of Rome's alliance relative to the treaty decides who broke faith — and it cannot be settled." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-zama", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "No Longer an Empire", era: "202 – 183 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Carthage survived the Second Punic War. What it did not survive was the peace terms — and the clause that killed it looks, at first glance, like the least dramatic one in the document.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The terms",
      text: ["The defeat at **Zama** in 202 BC ended the war. The battle itself is in the **Second Punic War** entry under Wars. What concerns us here is the settlement, which was built less to punish Carthage than to make it permanently incapable.",
        "Carthage kept its African territory and lost everything else — Spain, the islands, every overseas possession. It handed over its elephants and promised to train no more. It surrendered its navy down to ten triremes; Livy describes the remaining five hundred ships being towed out past the harbour mouth and burned in full view of the city, and says the people watching from the walls mourned as though Carthage itself were alight. It accepted an indemnity of ten thousand talents over fifty years, and gave up a hundred hostages against it.",
        "And then it accepted one clause that mattered more than all of the rest put together. **Carthage would make no war outside Africa at all, and none inside Africa without Rome's permission.**",
        "When the terms were read out at home, a senator named Gisgo stood up to argue against accepting them — and Hannibal, who had actually lost the battle and knew exactly what the alternative looked like, physically grabbed him and hauled him down off the platform. The assembly was scandalised. He apologised, saying he had left Carthage at nine years old and had learned a soldier's manners rather than a citizen's, and then told them to accept, and to be grateful the terms were not worse.",
        "Notice what Rome had not done. It annexed nothing. It left a wealthy trading city standing with its constitution, its territory and its commerce entirely intact, and removed only its capacity to defend them.",
        "A state that cannot go to war without another state's consent is a client, whatever the paperwork says. That clause was not a humiliation to be lived down over time. It was a mechanism, and it took fifty years to finish running."],
      key: "The 202 BC terms stripped Carthage of empire, navy and the right to wage war without Roman permission." },
    { title: "Masinissa's licence",
      text: ["Then Rome made a king. **Masinissa** was a Numidian prince who had fought for Carthage in Spain, switched sides when the war turned, and brought the cavalry that broke Hannibal's flank at Zama. Rome confirmed him as king of a united Numidia along Carthage's landward border, and guaranteed him. He was about fifty at Zama. He reigned for another half century, which is a long time to hold a grudge with an army.",
        "That kingdom was Rome's other creation of 202 BC and the more durable one. Where there had been rival chiefdoms there was now a single Numidia, with a capital at Cirta, its own coinage, grain for export and an army drilled on the Roman model. Rome had not simply left a neighbour on Carthage's border. It had built one.",
        "Masinissa then spent his entire reign taking Carthaginian land, and the pattern never varied once. Numidian settlement or raiding pushes into a border district. Carthage protests to Rome, as the treaty obliges it to. A Roman commission comes out to arbitrate. The commission finds for Masinissa. The rich coastal Emporia went that way. So did the farmland of the Great Plains, and in the 150s BC some fifty or seventy towns. Cato himself came out on one of these commissions, and we will come back to that.",
        "Sit with the position Carthage was in, because it is genuinely airtight. It could not fight, because fighting without permission broke the treaty. It could not win an arbitration, because the arbitrator had installed the other party. It could not appeal past Rome, because by this point there was no longer anybody else to appeal to.",
        "The permission clause had converted an ordinary border dispute into a legal trap. Resisting meant war with Rome. Not resisting meant losing the country in instalments. Every year Carthage complied was a year it got smaller, and the only door out of the trap led straight to the end."],
      key: "Rome guaranteed Masinissa's Numidia, which annexed Carthaginian land for fifty years under Roman arbitration." },
    { title: "Hannibal's second career",
      text: ["Here is the part of the story I think most people never hear, and it is the best thing in the chapter.",
        "**Hannibal** did not flee after Zama, and he did not retire to his estates to write memoirs. In 196 BC, six years after the worst defeat of his life, he stood for office, was elected **suffete** — one of the two annual chief magistrates — and turned on his own governing class.",
        "The Council of 104, the judicial body that had shadowed and second-guessed Carthaginian generals for two centuries, held office for life and answered to nobody. Hannibal made it annually elected and barred consecutive terms. When a quaestor from the judges' faction ignored a summons, Hannibal had him arrested, dragged him in front of the popular assembly, and used the occasion to go after the entire order.",
        "Then he opened the accounts. And this is the killer. The revenues of the state, he demonstrated, were more than enough to pay Rome's indemnity without a single new tax. The money was simply being stolen — in quantity — by precisely the men who had been complaining loudest about the burden.",
        "The reforms worked. Within a few years Carthage offered to pay off the entire remaining fifty-year indemnity in one lump sum. Rome refused. Of course it refused: a Carthage out of debt is a Carthage out of reach.",
        "His enemies at home, whose incomes he had just ended, wrote to Rome accusing him of conspiring with **Antiochus III** of Syria for a new war. A Roman commission arrived to investigate. Rather than let his city be forced to hand him over, Hannibal left Carthage at night, rode to a tower he kept on the coast, and sailed for Tyre. He served Antiochus, then Crete, then **Prusias of Bithynia** — always useful, always a bargaining chip — until Rome asked for him one final time. At **Libyssa**, around 183 BC, with the house surrounded, he took the poison he had carried for years, remarking that the Roman people could not be bothered to wait for an old man to die.",
        "He beat Rome in the field for sixteen years and was finished off by the accountants of his own city, who used Rome as the instrument. Carthage's ruling class preferred a diminished city they controlled to a strong one they did not. Thirty-seven years later that preference left the city with nobody able to defend it."],
      key: "Hannibal reformed Carthage's finances as suffete, was denounced by his own oligarchy, and died in exile c. 183 BC." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Which peace term mattered most in the long run?", options: ["The loss of elephants", "The ban on waging war without Rome's permission", "The loss of Sicily", "The surrender of hostages"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It made self-defence against Masinissa legally impossible — the mechanism that eventually produced the Third Punic War." },
    { q: "Why did Rome refuse Carthage's offer to pay the indemnity early?", options: ["The sum was too small", "A debt-free Carthage would be an independent one", "Rome had lost the treaty", "Carthage lacked the money"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The indemnity was leverage as much as compensation, so an early settlement was against Roman interests." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-end", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "Delenda Est", era: "149 – 146 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Carthage recovered commercially, complied legally, and kept every clause of the treaty. It was destroyed anyway.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The recovery that condemned it",
      text: ["By the 150s BC the indemnity was paid off and Carthage was visibly, conspicuously rich again. No empire, no navy, no wars. Just the harbour, the African hinterland, and fifty years of trade uninterrupted by the enormous expense of defending anything.",
        "And that was the problem. Roman envoys who went out to arbitrate Masinissa's latest land grab came home unsettled by what they had seen: a full harbour, crowded streets, timber and iron stacked in the yards, a countryside of orchards and estates.",
        "**Cato the Elder** had been on one of those embassies. Pliny tells us that he came into the Senate afterwards, shook a fresh African fig out of a fold of his toga onto the floor, and asked the house how far away the country was that had grown it. Three days' sail, he told them. It is a magnificent piece of political theatre — the fig is still fresh, therefore the enemy is still close — and it worked.",
        "From then on, whatever the debate happened to be about, Cato is said to have finished every speech he made by declaring that Carthage must be destroyed. The famous Latin phrase *Carthago delenda est* is a later formulation rather than a quotation, so the app files the wording as **Traditional / Legendary**. The campaign itself is **Established**.",
        "He did not have the Senate to himself, though, and the man arguing against him deserves more attention than he usually gets. **Scipio Nasica** argued, repeatedly and for years, that Rome needed a rival to stay honest — that fear of Carthage was the discipline holding the Roman state together, and that removing it would turn Roman aggression inward.",
        "He lost the argument. Within two generations Rome was fighting itself."],
      key: "Carthage's commercial recovery alarmed Rome; Cato's campaign to destroy it is established, the famous phrasing is not." },
    { title: "The trap closes",
      text: ["In 149 BC Carthage finally did the forbidden thing. Masinissa's raids had been running for half a century. Carthage raised an army under a general named Hasdrubal, marched out to stop him without asking Rome first, and was beaten. So it had now lost twice in one campaign: in the field, and in the legal cover it had spent fifty years carefully protecting.",
        "Rome declared war and landed an army at Utica, which had already changed sides. And then the demands began — and watch the order of them, because the order is the whole point.",
        "First: three hundred hostages, children of the noble families, to be delivered at Lilybaeum. Carthage delivered them. The accounts dwell on the scenes at the harbour as the ships took them off.",
        "Next: all weapons and war material. Carthage handed over what is reported as two hundred thousand sets of armour and two thousand catapults, and the consuls praised the city for its obedience.",
        "Only then — with the hostages gone and the arsenals empty — did the consul **Censorinus** deliver the last demand. The Carthaginians were to leave their city and settle somewhere at least ten miles inland, away from the sea. The site would be destroyed. The people would not be harmed.",
        "For a nation whose entire existence — trade, revenue, religion, identity — ran through a harbour, that is not relocation. That is extinction with a friendly face on it, and the envoys understood it as such the moment they heard it.",
        "Rome took the compliance first and made the impossible demand last. That is the order you use when what you actually want is the refusal, not the surrender."],
      key: "Rome extracted hostages and weapons, then demanded Carthage abandon its coastal city entirely; Carthage refused." },
    { title: "146 BC",
      text: ["Carthage, with no weapons, no navy and no allies, refused. And then held out for three years.",
        "The city turned itself into one enormous workshop. Public buildings were stripped for metal and temples for timber, and the accounts claim a daily output of a hundred and forty shields, three hundred swords, five hundred spears and a thousand catapult bolts — with women cutting off their hair to make cordage for the torsion springs. The siege itself, the mole thrown across the harbour mouth, the blockade and the starvation, is in the **Third Punic War** entry under Wars.",
        "**Scipio Aemilianus** — adopted grandson of the Scipio who had beaten Hannibal, which is a tidy piece of historical symmetry — took the walls in the spring of 146 BC. The fighting went street by street and roof by roof for six days, up the terraces towards the citadel of Byrsa. The Carthaginian commander Hasdrubal came out and surrendered himself; his wife, watching from the temple roof with the last of the defenders, is said to have called down that he had bought his own life at the price of his city, and then killed her children and walked into the fire.",
        "Perhaps fifty thousand survivors were sold into slavery, and the site was methodically demolished. And while we are here: the story that Rome ploughed salt into the fields has **no ancient source at all**. None. It appears to be a nineteenth-century invention, and it is one of the most widely repeated false facts in ancient history.",
        "Polybius was standing next to Scipio while the city burned, and records that Scipio wept and quoted Homer on the fall of Troy — not out of pity for Carthage, but because he had just realised that the same thing would one day be done to Rome.",
        "A century later Rome founded a colony on the same ground. It became one of the great cities of the empire and, eventually, the see of Augustine. The place Rome erased is the place Rome rebuilt."],
      key: "Carthage fell in 146 BC after a three-year siege; the salting of the fields is a modern invention with no ancient source." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was Rome's final demand before the war?", options: ["A larger indemnity", "The surrender of Hannibal", "That Carthage abandon its coastal city and move inland", "Conversion to Roman religion"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Framed as a relocation, and functioning as an extinction order for a maritime trading society." },
    { q: "How should 'Rome salted the fields of Carthage' be classified?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "A modern invention with no ancient source", "Traditional / Legendary from Polybius"], correct: 2,
      explain: "No ancient writer mentions it at all; the story enters the record in the nineteenth century." },
  ],
},
);
